---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { meta } from "../../data/pages";
const submissionURL = "https://github.com/torana-edge/torana-site/issues/new?template=plugin-listing.yml";
---
<Layout {...meta("/plugins/submit/")}>
  <article class="frame prose">
    <h1 class="display">Your workflow hack could help someone else.</h1>
    <p>A small plugin that solves one annoying problem is a good place to start. Try moving a harness-specific tool or policy into Torana so you can reuse it across compatible setups. It does not need to cover everyone’s workflow.</p>
    <h2>Build it where you like.</h2>
    <p>Start with the <a href="/docs/plugin-authoring/">Go and Rust plugin-authoring guide</a> to scaffold, build, and test your plugin. The <a href="https://github.com/torana-edge/torana-plugin-sdk">SDK repository</a> has helpers and further examples. Your source can stay in your own repository. People can <a href="/docs/plugin-installation/#community">install it from source</a> without a central publishing step or a catalogue listing.</p>
    <p>Plugins work with a <a href="/docs/support/#shared-format">shared request format</a>, but compatibility still depends on the fields and capabilities they use. Document the harnesses, endpoints, and versions you have actually tried.</p>
    <h2>Request a place in the catalogue.</h2>
    <p>Open a listing request in the website repository. Include:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The problem it solves and a small example of it working.</li>
      <li>A public source link, license, and the tag or commit to review.</li>
      <li>Build and installation instructions, plus any configuration it needs.</li>
      <li>Requested permissions and resources, including where data goes if it uses network access.</li>
      <li>The Torana and SDK versions, harnesses, and provider endpoints you tested.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href={submissionURL}>Request a listing ↗</a></p>
    <p>Maintainers review requests before adding a listing and may ask for changes or more information. Keep credentials, private prompts, and customer data out of the issue and examples.</p>
    <h2>A listing helps people find it.</h2>
    <p>It is not a security certification or automatic approval. A listing only helps people find the source; they still choose it explicitly, build it locally, and approve the permissions of the exact bundle they run.</p>
    <p>Community listings identify the reviewed revision, not future branch updates. Maintainers may remove listings if source disappears, maintenance or compatibility lapses, licensing changes, or security concerns emerge.</p>
    <p>Not looking for a listing? Share your repository directly. The install path works the same way.</p>
    <p><a href="/plugins/">Browse existing plugins →</a></p>
  </article>
</Layout>
